 The area of a trapezoid is given by the formula: \[
\text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times (\text{Base}_1 + \text{Base}_2) \times \text{Height}
\] In this case, one base is twice the height ($2x$) and the other base is three times the height ($3x$), and the height is $x$. So, we can substitute these values into the formula: \[
\text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times (2x + 3x) \times x = \frac{1}{2} \times 5x \times x = \frac{1}{2} \times 5x^2 = \frac{5x^2}{2}
\] </think> The answer is: \[
\boxed{\frac{5x^2}{2}}
